#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205241 :tutorial/content "The multiplicative inverse of an integer $a$ modulo $n$ is the unique number $x$ such that\n\n$$\na \\cdot x \\equiv 1 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\:n).\n$$\n\nThe word \"unique\" in the statement above means with respect to the inequality $0 \\leq x < n.$ \n\nFor example, $x={\\color{red}{4}}$ is the multiplicative inverse of $a={\\color{blue}{6}}$ modulo $23$ since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\cdot 4 & ≡24 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,23) \\\\  & ≡23+1 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,23) \\\\  & ≡0+1 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,23) \\\\  & ≡1 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,23) & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following: \n\n- We sometimes denote the multiplicative inverse of $a$ as $a^{-1}.$ So, ${\\color{blue}{6}}^{-1} = {\\color{red}{4}}$ in this example.\n\n- Be careful not confuse the notation $a^{-1}$ with the reciprocal of $a.$ In this context, both $a$ and $a^{-1}$ are *integers.*\n\n- The uniqueness of $a^{-1}$ is up to congruence modulo $n.$ For example, since ${\\color{red}{27}}\\equiv {\\color{red}{4}}\\:(\\textrm{mod }23),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}27\\cdot 6 & ≡162 & (\\text{mod}\\,23) \\\\  & ≡7\\cdot 23+1 & (\\text{mod}\\,23) \\\\  & ≡7\\cdot 0+1 & (\\text{mod}\\,23) \\\\  & ≡1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,23) & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are circumstances when a multiplicative inverse does not exist. To this end, we have the following theorem:\n\n*An integer $a$ has a multiplicative inverse modulo $n$ if and only if $a$ and $n$ are coprime, i.e., $\\textrm{gcd}(a,n)=1.$*\n\nFor example:\n\n- We saw earlier that $6^{-1} = 4$ modulo $23.$ Indeed, $\\textrm{gcd}(6,23) = 1.$ Moreover, since $n=23$ is prime, *every* integer $1 \\leq x \\leq 22$ modulo $23$ has a multiplicative inverse. More on that in future lessons.\n\n- In contrast, the integer $a=2$ has no multiplicative inverse modulo $14$ since $\\textrm{gcd}(2,14) = 2\\neq 1.$ Intuitively, multiplying $a = 2$ by any integer $x$ modulo $14$ gives an even number, and taking the result modulo $14$ (also even) will never give us $1$ (an odd integer)." :tutorial/id #uuid "0f17a19d-6a8b-5bc3-8791-dea4766e234f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9080 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205249 :tutorial/content "Now, suppose we want to compute the multiplicative inverse of $23$ modulo $79.$ \n\nSince $\\textrm{gcd}(23,79)=1,$ the inverse exists. However, the modulus $n=79$ is quite big, so finding the inverse by trial and error is inefficient. So, what do we do?\n\nTo answer this, recall that Bézout's identity states that there exist integers $u$ and $v$ such that\n\n$$\n23u + 79v = \\textrm{gcd}(23,79).\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\textrm{gcd}(23,79)=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n23u + 79v = 1.\n$$\n\nWe can find the integers $u$ and $v$ using the extended Euclidian algorithm.  \n\nApplying the Euclidian algorithm (we'll skip over the details for now), we find that $u=-24, v = 7.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n23 \\cdot (-24) + 7 \\cdot 79 = 1.\n$$\n\nTo find the multiplicative inverse of $23,$ the key is to write this result modulo $79,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}23\\cdot (-24)+7\\cdot 79 & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,79) \\\\ 23\\cdot (-24)+7\\cdot 0 & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,79) \\\\ 23\\cdot (-24) & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,79) \\\\ 23\\cdot (0-24) & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,79) \\\\ 23\\cdot (79-24) & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,79) \\\\ 23\\cdot 55 & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,79)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $23 \\cdot 55 \\equiv 1 \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:79).$ Therefore, $55$ is the multiplicative inverse of $23$ (modulo $79$).\n\nFinally, we can express this result using the residue function as follows:\n\n$$\n23^{-1} \\: \\textrm{mod} \\: 79 = 55\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "447cbeda-3f33-51d7-89d9-c3319b9a824a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9110 :tutorial/title "Using the Extended Euclidean Algorithm"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205255 :tutorial/content "Suppose we wish to calculate the multiplicative inverse of $3$ modulo $14.$ In other words, we want to find a number $x$ such that\n\n$$\n3 \\cdot x \\equiv 1 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\:14).\n$$\n\nFirstly, we note that since $\\textrm{gcd}(3,14) = 1,$ a unique multiplicative inverse exists. \n\nSince the modulus $n=14$ is fairly small, one way to efficiently compute the inverse is to use trial and error. To do this, we compute powers of $3$ and take the residue modulo $14$ until we get a result of $1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{2} & ≡3\\cdot 3≡9 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,14) \\\\ {3}^{3} & ≡3\\cdot {3}^{2}≡3\\cdot 9≡27≡1\\cdot 14+13≡13 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,14) \\\\ {3}^{4} & ≡3\\cdot {3}^{3}≡3\\cdot 13≡39≡2\\cdot 14+11≡11 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,14) \\\\ {3}^{5} & ≡3\\cdot {3}^{4}≡3\\cdot 11≡33≡2\\cdot 14+5≡5 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,14) \\\\ {3}^{6} & ≡3\\cdot {3}^{5}≡3\\cdot 5≡15≡1\\cdot 14+1≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,14)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the last line, we have\n\n$$\n3 \\cdot {\\color{magenta}{5}}   \\equiv 1\\qquad  (\\textrm{mod}\\:14).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the multiplicative inverse of $3$ modulo $14$ is ${\\color{magenta}{5}},$ and we can write \n\n$$\n3^{-1} = {\\color{magenta}{5}}\\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\:14).\n$$\n\n**Note:** We could get the same result by substituting $x=1,2,\\ldots, 13$ into \n\n$$\n3 \\cdot x \\equiv 1 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\:14)\n$$\n\nuntil we get the correct congruence relation. However, the exponentiation method described above typically allows us to find the solution with fewer trials since the multiplicative inverse of $a$ (if exists) must appear among the powers of $a$ modulo $n.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "13c4f6aa-637a-5936-8a92-312c2b9e9a0a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9128 :tutorial/title "Finding a Multiplicative Inverse Using Trial and Error"}]}
